  "transcript": "# Fiscal devolution in England\n\n## Does fiscal devolution raise growth?\n> There is some evidence devolution helps growth, but causation is unclear and outcomes will vary by place.\n\nChair: First: will fiscal devolution boost economic growth, and will it help all places equally?\nAndrew Carter: There is some international evidence—wages and productivity are slightly higher where powers are local—but causation is hard to prove. Devolution is one useful intervention, not a miracle cure, and it may be better than the current, highly centralised status quo.\nStephen Jones: The tricky part is the counterfactual: separating what local decisions did from broader economic change. The stronger evidence is about capability—places where local leaders respond quickly and coordinate with businesses and universities tend to get better results.\nCouncillor Vince Maple: It’s not the fiscal power itself but better local decisions, closer relationships and being able to spot and prevent problems early. Places start from different positions; devolution helps some levers but won’t erase long-term deprivation on its own.\n\n\n## Replacing grants with tax shares: short-term certainty and equalisation\n> Swapping some grant for income-tax shares can give incentives, but needs predictable equalisation so places aren’t penalised unfairly.\n\nChair: Is what ministers propose really devolution, or just reshuffling central grants with a new formula?\nAndrew Carter: It’s more than a formula swap. Replacing fixed grants with a share of local tax links funding to local growth, giving places more flexibility and some upside if their tax base expands. But you must manage short-term certainty during transition.\nCouncillor Vince Maple: Places will worry about parity: many big towns still lack MSAs. Certainty matters; a two- to three-year integrated settlement replaced by an equivalent tax share could give short-term predictability.\nLord Young of Cookham: How can we tell if extra local receipts came from local choices or from national economic growth?\nAndrew Carter: You can’t fully disentangle that. The solution is designing mechanisms that reward growth while accepting some noise from the national economy, and testing arrangements over a couple of years.\nStephen Jones: Equalisation needs to be predictable and ongoing. Big resets every five years remove incentives for long-term investment. Models like a small top-slice or a baseline floor keep incentives while protecting places.\nAndrew Carter: A Swiss-style benchmark—pooling a share of growth above a threshold and redistributing it—can encourage ambition yet support those lagging behind. It’s feasible and has precedents.\n\n\n## Who should hold what powers: MSAs, councils and communities?\n> Strategic economic decisions fit MSAs, local services fit councils; one size won’t suit every area and roles must be clear.\n\nChair: Should major powers go to MSAs or stay with local authorities?\nAndrew Carter: The principle is economic coterminosity: strategic transport, planning and skills work at a wider economic geography, not at small borough level. MSAs make sense there; elsewhere, unitary councils are the right model.\nStephen Jones: We should design four-tier relationships—central, strategic, local and neighbourhood—so each can act where appropriate. The system must build subsidiarity, accountability and joined-up incentives rather than siloed decision-making.\nCouncillor Vince Maple: Power should sit where it needs to. Many district councils that drive local economies must be heard. Key Cities members outside MSAs want access to powers too; one size doesn’t fit a coastal town and a big urban centre.\nStephen Jones: There’s a real risk of duplication for national businesses or investors if every mayor acts separately. Groups like Core Cities exist to coordinate cities’ voices and avoid that.\n\n\n## Accountability, politics and the mayor’s role\n> Mayors raise public awareness and can resolve strategic deadlocks, but call-in powers and majority voting raise tensions that need careful safeguards.\n\nChair: Does concentrating power in mayors risk imposing partisan decisions on parts of a city that disagree?\nStephen Jones: It depends on the decision. For genuinely strategic issues that affect the whole city-region—say a major transport or retail artery—there’s legitimacy for mayoral involvement. But mayors acting as a habitual override would be counterproductive.\nAndrew Carter: Mayors increase public clarity: polling shows people in mayoral areas know who their mayor is far more than they know their council leader. Call-in powers should be last resort, used sparingly to resolve strategic impasses.\nCouncillor Vince Maple: The main concern locally is powers moving up from councils to mayors, especially on planning. Some leaders fear losing local say; others accept mayors can help attract investment.\nStephen Jones: Legally, combined authority decisions are taken by the authority (mayor plus council leaders) with majority voting and the mayor often holding a casting vote. In practice, effective places work by ongoing collaboration, not constant voting.\nAndrew Carter: If voters dislike the outcomes they can change the mayor or councils at election time. But there’s a design challenge: you need some downside to local decisions so incentives remain, while having emergency national backstops for major failure.\n\n\n## Capacity, timing and a pragmatic road map\n> Devolution needs transfers of staff, steady funding, predictable equalisation and clarity on roles; a phased roadmap and capacity-building matter more than instant universal rollout.\n\nChair: Are local councils ready for these changes? Will capacity be sucked into mayoral offices?\nCouncillor Vince Maple: Local officers are resilient and talented, but the Government’s pause on reorganisation has left staff uncertain. Some will move to new strategic roles, others will stay; the transition must be managed so services don’t suffer.\nStephen Jones: We must do organised workforce and organisational design: identify what skills are needed where, transfer headcount and budgets where appropriate, and avoid simply shifting people from one public job to another without net gain.\nAndrew Carter: Think system-wide: central departments should shrink where responsibilities move outwards. National agencies—Homes England, UKRI, etc.—also need to redeploy staff into places. The future test is not how many Whitehall officials remain, but how well places are supported.\nCouncillor Vince Maple: Practical unfairness already exists: examples like apprenticeship brokerage funding going only to MSAs leave unitary and non-MSA areas feeling they miss out. That undermines trust in the rollout.\nStephen Jones: The Budget is a stepping stone, not the finish. Revenue shares, business-rate reform and motoring taxes are all areas for further, careful design. A roadmap that builds capability, predictable rules and a modest equalisation top-slice will keep incentives for growth.\nAndrew Carter: A practical metric: increase the share of locally-raised tax retained locally from roughly 5% today towards 10–15% over time. That would be meaningful but still cautious.\nCouncillor Vince Maple: Above all, clarify what 'fiscal devolution' actually means for each place, co-produce the design with local government, and fix outdated things like council tax to modernise the tax base.\nChair: Thank you all. The evidence is clear: devolution can help but needs predictable rules, clarity on roles, built capacity and fair equalisation to avoid making some places worse off.\n",
